<h2>Indian soybean meal (de-oiled cake)</h2>
<p>Soybean meal is the protein backbone of most commercial poultry, aqua and dairy feed formulations. India's position in this trade is specific and worth understanding as a buyer: Indian meal generally costs more than Brazilian, Argentine or US product, and buyers choose it anyway for one of two reasons — non-GMO status, or freight advantage into South Asia, East Africa and parts of the Gulf.</p>

<p>We supply 46% and 48% minimum protein solvent-extracted meal from plants in Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra and Rajasthan, bagged or in jumbo bags.</p>

<h2>Why does non-GMO status matter?</h2>
<p>India does not permit commercial cultivation of genetically modified soybean, so Indian soybean meal is conventional by origin rather than by certification scheme. For European feed buyers serving non-GMO-labelled meat, poultry and dairy supply chains, that origin characteristic is the reason to look at India at all — it's a structural advantage no amount of price competition from South America changes.</p>
<p>We can supply a non-GMO declaration with shipments. Note that this is a statement about Indian soybean cultivation, not a certification claim under any particular scheme — if your buyer requires a specific certification standard, tell us up front so we can be honest about whether we can meet it.</p>

<h2>What should I specify on a soybean meal order?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Crude protein minimum</strong> — 46% or 48%, and whether on a dry or as-is basis.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture maximum</strong> — typically ≤12%. Higher moisture is both a weight and a storage-stability issue.</li>
  <li><strong>Sand/silica maximum</strong> — typically ≤2%. This is the adulteration parameter to watch on cheap offers.</li>
  <li><strong>Urease activity</strong> — indicates proper heat treatment during processing; under-processed meal contains anti-nutritional factors, over-processed meal has reduced protein digestibility.</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong> — 25/40/50kg bags, jumbo bags, or bulk, depending on your mill's intake handling.</li>
</ul>

